<h2>Four quick ways to tell which you are</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>The push test.</strong> Stand with your feet together and have a friend give you a gentle, unexpected push from behind. The foot you step forward with to catch yourself is usually your front foot.</li>
  <li><strong>The slide test.</strong> Take a running start in socks on a smooth floor and slide. Whichever foot ends up in front is a strong hint.</li>
  <li><strong>The stairs test.</strong> Notice which foot you naturally lead with when you start walking up stairs. Many people lead with their front foot.</li>
  <li><strong>Board sports you already do.</strong> If you skateboard, surf, or wakeboard, use the same stance. Your body has already decided.</li>
</ol>
<p>If the tests disagree, go with what happens most often, then confirm on snow. After a few runs, most beginners know right away whether they're on the wrong foot.</p>

<h2>Handedness isn't a reliable guide</h2>
<p>Being right-handed doesn't make you regular, and being left-handed doesn't make you goofy. Footedness and handedness often differ, which is why the push and slide tests are more useful than asking which hand you write with.</p>

<h2>How to set your bindings for your stance</h2>
<p>Your stance determines which binding goes in front. For regular riders, the left binding goes toward the nose; for goofy riders, the right one does. Rental shops will ask your stance and set this for you.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Stance width:</strong> roughly shoulder width, measured from the center of one binding to the other.</li>
  <li><strong>Angles:</strong> a common beginner setup is a slight forward angle on the front binding and a smaller or zero angle on the back. A "duck" stance, with the toes pointed slightly outward on both feet, makes riding in either direction easier.</li>
  <li><strong>Check after a few runs:</strong> if turns feel forced in one direction, ask the shop to double-check angles and stance width.</li>
</ul>
<p>For more on binding setup, read <%= link_to "how to choose snowboard bindings", guide_path("best-snowboard-bindings") %>.</p>

<h2>What "switch" means</h2>
<p>Riding <strong>switch</strong> means riding with your other foot forward: a regular rider riding goofy, or the reverse. It's a core skill for park riding, spins, and landing tricks backward. Twin-shaped boards, which are symmetrical nose to tail, ride the same in both directions and make switch easier. Learn your natural stance first, then practice switch on easy terrain once you can link turns comfortably.</p>

<h2>Picking your first board and lessons</h2>
<p>Once you know your stance, the next steps are getting the right length board and learning the basics. Use the <%= link_to "snowboard size calculator", snowboard_size_chart_path %> to find your size, and read <%= link_to "how to snowboard", guide_path("how-to-snowboard") %> for your first day. Soft, freshly groomed snow makes learning much easier, so check <%= link_to "where it's snowing this week", where_is_it_snowing_path %> before you book.</p>
<% faqs << { question: "Is goofy or regular better for snowboarding?", answer: "Neither is better. Regular (left foot forward) is more common, but goofy riders perform exactly the same. Ride whichever stance feels natural." } %>
<% faqs << { question: "How do I know if I'm goofy or regular?", answer: "Have a friend give you a gentle push from behind and see which foot you step forward with, or slide on a smooth floor in socks. The foot that ends up in front is usually your front foot on a snowboard." } %>
<% faqs << { question: "Does being left-handed make you goofy?", answer: "Not necessarily. Handedness and footedness often differ, so use a push or slide test rather than your writing hand." } %>
<% faqs << { question: "Can I switch from regular to goofy later?", answer: "Yes. If your stance feels wrong after a few runs, have the bindings swapped. Many experienced riders also learn to ride switch, which means riding with the other foot forward." } %>
